<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saqaliba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saqaliba, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>', was the Arabic term for slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> of European origin in the medieval Islamic world, many taken from Slavic lands. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> they served as palace officials and soldiers of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and some rose to high command. After the caliphate collapsed, Saqaliba leaders founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms of their own, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Denia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saqaliba.jpg" alt="Saqaliba" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Ivanov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._V._Ivanov._Trade_negotiations_in_the_country_of_Eastern_Slavs._Pictures_of_Russian_history._(1909).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
